# Chapter 10 · Nature versus Culture: A Debate in Gender Development

> The syllabus calls it "Nature versus Culture: A Debate in Gender Development". The examiner asked it in 2018, joined to the sex/gender debate ("reflecting the nature versus nurture argument"). You need both sides with evidence, one famous case, the anthropologists' view, and a reasoned conclusion.

## 10.1  The debate stated

::: define Nature versus culture (nurture) | the central debate of gender development
The debate over whether differences between women and men — in behaviour, abilities and roles — come mainly from **nature** (genes, hormones, brain, evolution) or mainly from **culture** (upbringing, socialisation, social structures and expectations).
-> The debate matters because it decides whether gender inequality is fixed or can be changed.
:::

"**Gender development**" means the process by which a child comes to have a gender identity, gendered behaviour and gendered roles. The question is: what drives that process?

## 10.2  The case for nature

1. **Chromosomes and hormones.** Sex chromosomes (XX, XY) direct the growth of the body's reproductive system. Hormones — especially **testosterone** before birth and at puberty — shape the body and, many researchers argue, some behaviour.
2. **Evidence from a hormone condition.** Girls with **congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH)** are exposed to high levels of male hormones before birth. On average they show a greater liking for toys usually preferred by boys (studies by Sheri Berenbaum and Melissa Hines, from 1992).
3. **Average differences.** In many societies men are, on average, physically stronger and more physically aggressive. Some studies report small average differences in certain spatial or verbal tasks.
4. **Evolution.** Scholars such as **E. O. Wilson** (*Sociobiology*, ==1975==) and **David Buss** argue that evolution gave men and women different ways of reproducing successfully, and that these still shape behaviour.
5. **A biological theory of crime.** The **evolutionary neuroandrogenic theory** (Lee Ellis) says that evolution and male hormones (androgens) explain why men commit more violent crime in every society. A 2018 MCQ called this "the belief that gender differences are universal".

## 10.3  The case for culture

1. **Differences between cultures.** Margaret Mead's three New Guinea societies (==1935==) showed "masculine" and "feminine" qualities shared out very differently (Chapter 6).
2. **Change over history.** In the World Wars, women did "men's work" in factories and fields. In two generations, women in many countries went from exclusion to being most university students. Biology did not change in that time; society did.
3. **Adults treat babies differently.** In the "Baby X" experiments of the 1970s (for example Condry and Condry, ==1976==), adults watched the same baby. When told it was a boy, they called its crying "anger"; when told it was a girl, they called it "fear".
4. **Socialisation is everywhere.** Pink and blue clothes, toys, chores, school subjects and television send children constant messages about gender (Chapter 6).
5. **The brain can change.** **Cordelia Fine** (*Delusions of Gender*, ==2010==) argued that many claimed brain differences are weak or badly measured, and that the brain changes with experience. **Daphna Joel** (==2015==) found that most brains are a "**mosaic**" of features, not neatly "male" or "female".

::: case The David Reimer case | the limits of "pure nurture"
In 1966 a Canadian baby boy, David Reimer, lost his penis in a failed circumcision. On the advice of the psychologist John Money, he was surgically changed and raised as a girl. Money reported the case as proof that gender identity is learnt. In fact Reimer never felt he was a girl. He returned to living as a male in his teens, and later spoke publicly about the harm done to him. He died in 2004.
-> The case warns against both extremes: gender identity was not simply made by upbringing — and neither doctors nor theories should force a gender on a person.
:::

## 10.4  The anthropologists' view: nature and culture as a gendered pair

::: thinker Sherry Ortner | American anthropologist · "Is Female to Male as Nature Is to Culture?" (1974)
Ortner asked why women are valued less in almost every society. Her answer: every culture sees itself as rising above nature. Women — because of their bodies (periods, childbirth, breastfeeding) and their place in the home — are seen as closer to nature, while men are linked with culture. So women are ranked lower, just as nature is ranked below culture.
-> The very idea that women are "closer to nature" is a cultural idea — so part of the "nature" side of the debate is really culture.
:::

## 10.5  The answer: interaction

Almost all scholars today reject both extremes. The **interactionist** (or "biosocial") view says:

- biology gives **tendencies and possibilities**, not a fixed destiny;
- culture **shapes, strengthens, weakens or reverses** those tendencies;
- the two act on each other throughout life — hormones affect behaviour, and experience affects hormones and the brain.

::: fig spectrum | Positions in the nature–culture debate.
@left Nature
@right Culture
8: Biology decides everything
28: Sociobiology and evolutionary psychology
50: Interaction (the modern view)
72: Social learning and socialisation theories
92: Strong social constructionism
:::

::: simple
Nature deals the cards; culture sets the rules of the game. Two players with similar cards can end up very differently depending on the rules — and the rules, unlike the cards, can be changed.
:::

## 10.6  Why the debate matters — especially for policy

::: table | What each side means for policy.
@widths 3,5.5,5.5
If … | It suggests … | The danger
Differences are mostly natural | Unequal roles cannot be avoided; policy should accept "natural" divisions | It can justify discrimination: "women are not fit for leadership, science or the army"
Differences are mostly cultural | Education, law and social change can remove unfair differences | It can ignore real bodily needs (pregnancy, maternal health, safety)
Nature and culture interact (the evidence) | Remove social barriers **and** meet bodily needs | —
:::

::: pakistan | The evidence from Pakistan
Pakistan's own experience supports the cultural and interactionist views. Women's literacy has risen from very low levels at independence to about ==53%== (Census 2023). Girls often top the board examinations. Women now serve as fighter pilots, Supreme Court judges, police officers and a provincial chief minister (since ==2024==). None of these changes needed a change in biology. They followed changes in education, law, opportunity and attitudes — showing how much of the "natural" order was social all along.
:::

::: eye | The 2018 essay: sex versus gender, reflecting nature versus nurture
Structure: (1) introduction — define sex, gender, nature and nurture; (2) the sex/gender distinction (Stoller, Oakley); (3) the nature argument, with evidence; (4) the nurture argument, with evidence (Mead, Baby X, Fine); (5) the David Reimer case — the limits of both extremes; (6) queer theory's question — is sex made too? (Chapter 8); (7) Ortner; (8) interaction; (9) policy and Pakistan; (10) conclusion. Draw the spectrum.
:::
